     This Protection does not consider `lookback_period` as it only looks at the latest trade.
 
+#### Max Consecutive Losses
+
+`MaxConsecutiveLosses` looks at the most recently closed trades - for a pair (`stop_per_pair`) and, unless disabled, across all pairs (`global_stop`) - and counts backwards from the latest one as long as trades keep closing with a profit below `required_profit`. Unlike `StoplossGuard` and `LowProfitPairs`, it does not use `lookback_period`: a single winning (or breakeven) trade resets the streak, no matter how long ago the losses happened.
+
+Once `trade_limit` or more losing trades have closed in an unbroken row, trading is locked for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`).
+
+By default, both the per-pair and the bot-wide (all pairs) checks are active, using the same `trade_limit` and `stop_duration`. Set `only_per_pair` to `true` to disable the bot-wide check and only guard individual pairs - useful when a strategy trades many uncorrelated pairs and a losing streak on one pair shouldn't pause the others.
+
+For futures bots, setting `only_per_side` will make the bot only consider one side, allowing for example shorts to continue after a losing streak of longs.
+
+The below example stops trading for a pair for 4 candles after 3 losing trades close in a row for that pair, and separately stops trading on all pairs for 4 candles after 5 losing trades close in a row bot-wide.
+
+``` python
+@property
+def protections(self):
+    return [
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "trade_limit": 3,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 4,
+            "required_profit": 0.0,
+            "only_per_pair": True,
+        },
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "trade_limit": 5,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 4,
+            "required_profit": 0.0,
+        },
+    ]
+```
+
 ### Full example of Protections
 
 All protections can be combined at will, also with different parameters, creating a increasing wall for under-performing pairs.
